1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,115
ReplayGuard began with a real Lambda and SQS failure on September
eighteenth.

2
00:00:05,115 --> 00:00:08,848
A simulated fulfillment succeeded, then the worker crashed.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,690
SQS delivered again.

4
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:18,629
The independently queried DynamoDB ledger recorded two receipts for the
vulnerable handler, and one for the repair.

5
00:00:18,629 --> 00:00:20,529
These are retained observations.

6
00:00:20,958 --> 00:00:23,800
The portable local lab asks a harder question.

7
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:25,932
Two valid orders share one product.

8
00:00:25,932 --> 00:00:31,657
Order A commits, loses its response, then retries among deliveries for
order B.

9
00:00:33,958 --> 00:00:37,291
Without a key, both orders have duplicate receipts.

10
00:00:37,291 --> 00:00:40,676
A product key looks safer, but suppresses order B.

11
00:00:40,676 --> 00:00:42,887
Zero receipts is also a failure.

12
00:00:43,958 --> 00:00:47,473
The stable business order key leaves one receipt for each order.

13
00:00:47,473 --> 00:00:52,172
The comparison checks both duplicate prevention and valid order
preservation.

14
00:00:52,958 --> 00:00:56,668
A small adapter connects your handler to the injected receiver.

15
00:00:56,668 --> 00:00:58,418
The handler chooses its key.

16
00:00:58,418 --> 00:01:05,811
The receiver owns the atomic receipt contract, and the observer counts
effects independently of the handler's return value.

17
00:01:07,958 --> 00:01:11,541
A pass belongs to the observed case and receiver assumptions.

18
00:01:11,541 --> 00:01:17,574
It does not promise an external shipment, payment, or safety for
arbitrary untrusted code.

19
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:24,547
Here is recorded command output from a fresh kit extraction, with
network access denied.

20
00:01:24,547 --> 00:01:28,097
The correct adapter produces one receipt and exits zero.

21
00:01:30,958 --> 00:01:35,017
Change the candidate to use no key, and run the same case again.

22
00:01:35,017 --> 00:01:36,186
Two receipts appear.

23
00:01:36,186 --> 00:01:39,466
The assertion fails, and the process exits one.

24
00:01:39,466 --> 00:01:41,273
This is actual execution.

25
00:01:43,958 --> 00:01:49,304
Restore the exact original source, and one receipt returns, with exit
zero.

26
00:01:49,304 --> 00:01:53,353
The recorded source hashes identify the changed and restored bytes.

27
00:01:55,958 --> 00:01:59,909
DispatchDesk uses a different business event and a separate adapter.

28
00:01:59,909 --> 00:02:04,584
Its author froze five cases before reading the evaluator implementation.

29
00:02:04,584 --> 00:02:08,672
Both authors were AI agents; this is not customer validation.

30
00:02:09,958 --> 00:02:12,072
All five reference cases pass.

31
00:02:12,072 --> 00:02:14,517
Removing the business key fails all five.

32
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:18,691
A product-only key fails the two cases with distinct valid orders.

33
00:02:19,958 --> 00:02:25,999
Download the kit, run trusted Python locally, change your handler, and
import the report.

34
00:02:25,999 --> 00:02:32,636
Inputs, fault conditions, assertions, source hashes, and receipt
evidence travel together.

35
00:02:33,042 --> 00:02:35,482
Missing observations remain incomplete.

36
00:02:35,482 --> 00:02:38,145
Unsupported guarantees remain unresolved.

37
00:02:38,145 --> 00:02:39,185
Keep the failure.

38
00:02:39,185 --> 00:02:40,203
Test the repair.
